timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr) said :

> So, how does it work?  How do you turn Federal Reserve Accounting Unit
> Devides (FRAUDs - their acronym, not mine) into digital cash?

I don't know exactly how this version works, but Chaum's basic system is :

1. You open an account with a 'bank' somewhere, and pay in some 'real' 
   money.
2. You send a crypto-signed message to the 'bank' asking for digital
   cash for some value, and it sends it to you, while reducing the
   cash in your account by the appropriate amount, and possibly
   taking a percentage.
3. You munge the resulting cash in some fashion to hide your identity.
4. You send it to the recipient.
5. They send it back to the 'bank' which then verifies it and adds
   the money to the recipient's account. They can either leave it there,
   or withdraw it as 'real' cash.

It's also possible to set things up so that you send 'real' money to
the 'bank' along with a key and anonymous remailing address, and it
sends you digital cash for that amount, or to buy digital cash off of
other people for 'real' cash. This avoids having to open an account in
the first place, and is essentially the system used in the freeware
(but probably patent-infringing) 'Magic Money' software.

'Bank' is in quotes since it doesn't have to be what is today regarded
as a bank, it can just be a computer in someone's bedroom, or a
street-corner drug-dealer's laptop PC and cellular modem, if you trust
them to look after your money.  Equally, 'opening an account' doesn't
neccesarily mean that you need to give them any kind of ID other than a
public key for verifying messages.

The main problem being local banking laws, which might well make such
arrangements illegal (not that it's likely to stop people from running
them). Of course, at least at this point, you really need a large bank
with a proven reputation to start issuing Digicash, and the last time I
checked the WWW server at digicash.support.nl there was no mention that
they had managed to get any such organisation interested. Until they do,
the answer to your question may well be "you don't".

			Mark

P.S. Apologies if the first two-thirds of the message is old news, I
wasn't sure whether you were talking about digital cash in general, or
this version in particular.

